#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06c1ce9a-bad7-50d6-b6c7-a1423ccce4bb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Geometric Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "f86e3e4f-53a7-5a77-a97a-e019ab8bc9aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-7500" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n\n- The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n\n- The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" :question/worked-solution "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the die is rolled before $4$ appears for the first time can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Rolling a die is a binomial experiment, but the number of times that $2$ appears cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Tossing a coin is a binomial experiment, and the number of times the coin is tossed before the first head appears can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157119 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88cf7c0e-8c1d-5a92-805f-0a81ee887546" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "e43bd196-6952-5cce-bc7b-7dccc06e3b57" :question/math-academy-id "e-7501" :question/problem "An archer is practicing archery. On average, the archer has a $7\\%$ chance of missing every shot he makes. What is the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shots until the archer misses. Since the chance of missing each shot is $7\\%,$ the number of shots  until the archer misses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 7\\% = \\dfrac{7}{100} = 0.07.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.07).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.07)^{x-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{x-1}(0.07)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the archer will miss for the first time on the $8$th shot is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=8) & =f(8) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{8-1}(0.07) \\\\  & =(0.93)^{7}(0.07) \\\\  & ≈0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157120 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cd0921-2ed0-5b05-985a-e1849e98da10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "04a21022-edc8-54c0-b5c9-232e600ac014" :question/math-academy-id "e-7502" :question/problem "A high school soccer team is playing a local tournament. On average, the team has a $10\\%$ chance of getting a tie in every game. What is the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%,$ the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.1)^{x-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{x-1}(0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{1-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{2-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{3-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)+(0.9)(0.1)+(0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =[1+0.9+(0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.271\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157121 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42f47d95-9f20-5488-98c4-cb80278a7cf3" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "69ee8489-db49-5121-9a0a-34da81a02c22" :question/math-academy-id "e-7503" :question/problem "A company is conducting interviews to fill a job. On average, $35\\%$ of applicants meet all the requirements. What is the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%,$ the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq  3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.725 \\\\  & ≈0.275\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06c1ce9a-bad7-50d6-b6c7-a1423ccce4bb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Geometric Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0531712-1b5c-5101-9300-5aaf93d6afdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156106} {:answer/id #uuid "d78434da-b286-5db8-be42-ebd420f0f4ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156107} {:answer/id #uuid "f2826afb-9c44-54e1-b04e-f697fb59f554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156108} {:answer/id #uuid "611a6edc-b687-5bbf-8b6b-6239d982f0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156109} {:answer/id #uuid "7adedf6f-d74b-51bb-9225-503927e7b775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156110}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a36469da-dc5c-504c-a914-43e2b3b82e12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156105}] :question/id #uuid "24647bed-66c6-5672-aafa-5b790ade6e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-85270" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n1. The number of questions answered correctly on a quiz before the first incorrect answer\n2. The cost of an item randomly selected from a store\n3. The number of products checked before finding a defective product"}]}
